## How Long Does Biotin Take to Strengthen Nails?

If biotin is going to help you, expect three to six months before you notice a real difference, and up to a year for full results. Nails grow slowly, roughly three millimetres a month, so no supplement can outrun that biology.

This timeline is not a marketing estimate. It reflects the nail growth cycle itself. A fingernail takes around six months to fully regrow from cuticle to tip, which means any structural improvement from increased keratin production has to grow out visibly before you can actually see it. The Swiss study that produced the well-known 25% thickness improvement measured its results at the six-month mark, not week two or week six. Anyone expecting visible change inside a month is working against basic nail physiology, regardless of dose.

This is also where most people quietly give up. They start a bottle in January, see nothing by March, and stop, right around the point where a genuinely deficient person might have started seeing early gains. If you are going to trial biotin, the only fair test is a consistent six-month run, tracked against a baseline photo, not a vague impression of "have my nails felt different lately."

If you are three months in with zero change, that is not necessarily a failure of patience. It may be your first real evidence that deficiency was never the issue.

